WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.137
Once the chunker works, we apply it to every source document.

00:00:04.137 --> 00:00:08.267
Each chunk becomes its own row in a new DataFrame.

00:00:08.267 --> 00:00:18.047
We give it a chunk ID and keep the original document ID, title, topic, permission, update date, and source.

00:00:18.047 --> 00:00:20.855
This is a very common RAG data model.

00:00:21.415 --> 00:00:26.827
The vector represents the chunk text, but the metadata travels with that vector.

00:00:26.827 --> 00:00:34.687
When retrieval returns a vector match, we need to know which source document it came from, what title to display,

00:00:34.687 --> 00:00:39.792
whether the user has permission to see it, and whether the source is current.

00:00:39.792 --> 00:00:44.987
Notice that we are not losing the relationship between chunk and document.

00:00:44.987 --> 00:00:52.062
One document may generate several chunks, but every chunk can still point back to the original source.

00:00:52.062 --> 00:00:58.863
That relationship becomes especially useful for citations, access control, and evaluation.
